Omar al-bashir is 75 he ruled Sudan for  three decades but today the former  dictator sat silently in a cage in a  Khartoum court to be told that he was  guilty of money laundering and  corruption he was charged after  suitcases containing millions of dollars  were discovered at his home earlier this  year the defendant Omar Hassan armored  al Bashir is sentenced to detention in a  reformed facility for a period of two  years his sentence was light it was  thought that he might get 10 years and  because of his age he won't go to a  normal prison where supporters protested  inside and outside the court  shouting in his defence Bashir became  president after a coup in 1989 he was  brutal thousands were killed under his  rule and opponents were jailed he was  finally removed from power earlier this  year when the military stepped in after  nationwide demonstrations  al-bashir is wanted by the International  Criminal Court in The Hague for war  crimes he's accused of genocide in the  state of Darfur the UN estimates 300,000  people were killed during the conflict  there but the joint civil-military  government that now rules Sudan has said  that he will not be extradited and  instead will remain in the country to  face judgment the people who've joined  the transitional government and want  them held to account for his part in the  killings of demonstrators in April April  this year before he was overthrown the  elements of the transitional military  government who are from the former  military regime would be very reluctant  to send one of their own two to the  Hague because the events in Darfur and  the sort of crimes that took place there  it would extend far beyond President  Bashir there is more to come  al Bashir faces outstanding trials in  Sudan which will get underway in the  coming months he will be back in court  and he will almost certainly face longer  in prison but after 30 years ruling  through violence that will still not be  justice enough for many sudanese  alistair bunk all Sky News
 

